Each of the six composition and fresh-replay Work figures has literal `N`/`N−1` tests; the parameter fixture also retains 37 prior Work on the same monotone ledger (`test:integration:lattice-cover-work`). Its witness and simultaneous staged-payload boundaries are 3,005 and 91,770 bytes, with literal refusal twins; 257 retained caller bytes raise the staged boundary to 92,027 (`test:integration:lattice-cover-capacities`). Every refusal restores the original graph counts and canonical snapshot without refunding Work. The cover uses ordinary witness and storage ceilings, and no default or existing scalar, basis or range pin changes (`ex:cas:lattice-cover-range-costs`), (`rule:cas:lattice-basis-capacity`).

**Example (Measured scalar baseline for lattice selection)** · `ex:cas:lattice-selection-scalar-baseline`

A temporary deliberately failing probe remeasured the original `10/Pi` enclosure at 57,786 Work, the nonnegative selection proof/fresh inspection at 166,952/247,365 Work with 1,562 witness bytes, and the bounded selection at 199,780/299,788 Work with 1,812 bytes. The existing literal `N`/`N−1` tests passed unchanged, and the probe was removed after measurement (`test:integration:integer-family-pi-costs`), (`test:integration:integer-family-pi-resources`), (`test:integration:decision-pi-resources`). These costs exclude source construction and keep the existing scalar owners separate from the later vector wrapper and affine cover of (`ex:cas:lattice-cover-range-costs`).

**Rule (Scalar enclosure reuse boundary for vector selection)** · `rule:cas:lattice-selection-enclosure-baseline`

The reusable coordinate grammar is `RationalEnclosure`, retaining the exact original target, context, actual input region, rational endpoints and checked replay origin (`def:cas:decision-enclosure-contract`). Closed rational normalization and Machin interval replay already check original operands and reciprocal domains (`rule:cas:decision-enclosure-replay`). A `CheckedRationalEnclosure` is local evidence without portable proof authority; the scalar selection certificate retains the complete enclosure in its ordinary witness (`rule:cas:decision-enclosure-authority`), (`rule:cas:integer-family-selection`). Ordered vector identity is implemented under (`rule:cas:lattice-original-box`); inverse-map box replay is implemented under (`rule:cas:lattice-affine-box`), (`rule:cas:lattice-coordinate-box`), while tuple selection still requires its checked composition under the root requirement “CAS original-target replay and tuple selection”.

**Rule (Original vector box contract)** · `rule:cas:lattice-original-box`

`RationalBoxEnclosure` names one nonempty explicit Real column, its exact context and actual input family, and an ordered `RationalEnclosure` for each original operand. `Cas::check_rational_box_enclosure` checks those identities and reuses scalar original-expression validation for every coordinate, returning a sealed `CheckedRationalBoxEnclosure` only after all coordinates pass (`test:integration:lattice-original-boxes`), (`def:cas:decision-enclosure-contract`), (`rule:cas:decision-enclosure-replay`). The receipt retains every exact origin and Machin certificate but creates no portable witness, local membership or tuple selection, preserving (`rule:cas:decision-enclosure-authority`).

**Rule (Vector box identity and atomic refusal)** · `rule:cas:lattice-original-box-refusal`

Changing the original vector, coordinate order, arity, scalar context or scalar input family refuses at the vector checker; a false final coordinate or unsupported origin refuses at the reused scalar checker (`test:integration:lattice-original-box-identities`), (`test:integration:lattice-original-box-refusals`). The read-only operation leaves graph counts and canonical snapshots unchanged and exposes no partial vector, including exhausted cumulative Work and insufficient returned ownership (`test:integration:lattice-original-box-work`), (`test:integration:lattice-original-box-output`). These checks establish the original-box portion of (`rule:cas:lattice-original-box`); affine transport and selection remain separate obligations.

**Example (Original vector box replay costs)** · `ex:cas:lattice-original-box-costs`

The wide torus box `[18,21] × [9,10]` costs 16,236 Work and its narrow twin `[19,20] × [9,10]` costs 16,300; each retains 1,840 logical bytes. The phase-wrap box `[15/2,8]`, wrap-straddling box `[6,13/2]` and scalar LC box `[203/64,205/64]` cost 56,902, 55,878 and 58,049 Work respectively, each retaining 1,592 bytes (`test:integration:lattice-original-box-costs`). Constant replay dominates these fixtures despite their smaller dimension. Each Work cost has a literal `N`/`N−1` check including 37 prior Work, and both returned sizes have literal output boundaries (`test:integration:lattice-original-box-work`), (`test:integration:lattice-original-box-output`). These are local original-box checks, without affine transport, proof construction or fresh witness replay; the existing scalar proof/replay pins of (`ex:cas:lattice-selection-scalar-baseline`) remain unchanged.

**Rule (Whole original-box affine replay)** · `rule:cas:lattice-affine-box`

`Cas::check_lattice_box_enclosure` checks a `LatticeBoxEnclosure` against the admitted original basis and exact homogeneous inverse, replays the original E through (`rule:cas:lattice-original-box`), and validates scalar enclosures of every inverse coefficient and translation entry. Exact outward products and sums check that the entire transformed E lies in the proposed H, retaining the determinant and both inverse proof roots (`test:integration:lattice-affine-boxes`), (`test:integration:lattice-affine-box-skew`). Irrational entries retain their exact expressions and checked Machin origins; an interval reciprocal meeting zero refuses even when the basis already has symbolic nonsingularity evidence (`test:integration:lattice-affine-box-reciprocal`), (`rule:cas:decision-enclosure-replay`). The returned `CheckedLatticeBoxEnclosure` is local evidence without portable vector or tuple-selection authority.

**Rule (Direct original-affine-expression replay)** · `rule:cas:lattice-coordinate-box`

`Cas::check_lattice_coordinate_enclosure` checks a `LatticeCoordinateEnclosure` against the original q and admitted inverse. Each declared coordinate expression must start at its exact inverse translation entry and add every ordered inverse-coefficient/original-coordinate product, including zero terms; scalar replay then validates its rational box (`test:integration:lattice-direct-coordinate-boxes`). This distinct form can prove exact phase cancellation and a sharper H without asserting containment of an earlier E. A standalone scalar surrogate or changed original q cannot replace that affine correspondence (`test:integration:lattice-direct-coordinate-correspondence`), (`test:integration:lattice-direct-coordinate-original-target`). The local receipt retains all original syntax, scalar origins and basis proof roots under (`rule:cas:lattice-basis-evidence`), and grants no portable vector witness or selected tuple.

**Rule (Complete coefficient reuse and affine refusals)** · `rule:cas:lattice-affine-box-refusal`

Multidimensional transport reuses scalar validation only for identical complete encoded contracts within that operation: original expression, context, family, endpoints, origin and full Machin data all belong to the charged key. The first occurrence, including an exact zero, still undergoes original-expression replay; changed repeated bounds, sums or origins refuse at their actual checker (`test:integration:lattice-affine-box-reuse-identity`). One-coordinate transforms retain direct scalar validation without reuse-key costs. Changed inverses, offsets, operand order or original targets refuse (`test:integration:lattice-affine-box-identities`), and replacing the wide torus transform by a sharper point box refuses (`test:integration:lattice-affine-box-whole-source`). These enforce whole-source containment in (`rule:cas:lattice-affine-box`) independently of the direct route in (`rule:cas:lattice-coordinate-box`).

**Example (Measured affine transport owners)** · `ex:cas:lattice-affine-box-costs`

Complete original-box transport costs 119,257 Work for the wide torus, 119,025 for its narrow twin, 131,645 for phase wrap, 130,661 for wrap straddle, 80,471 for scalar LC and 74,753 for the translated rational skew fixture. Returned ownership is respectively 8,728, 8,720, 4,508, 4,516, 3,948 and 7,648 logical bytes (`test:integration:lattice-affine-box-costs`). Each Work figure has a literal `N`/`N−1` check including 37 prior Work and unchanged graph snapshots (`test:integration:lattice-affine-box-work`). These are local enclosure checks after basis admission, including original replay; proof construction and fresh witness replay are not implemented by these receipts. Existing scalar and original-box pins remain unchanged (`ex:cas:lattice-selection-scalar-baseline`), (`ex:cas:lattice-original-box-costs`).

**Example (Measured coefficient replay and route choice)** · `ex:cas:lattice-enclosure-reuse-costs`

A temporary failing phase split measured 137,012 coefficient-validation Work within the original 184,357-Work wide-torus transform; complete-contract reuse removes 65,100 Work, while the one-coordinate paths retain their original costs (`ex:cas:lattice-affine-box-costs`), (`rule:cas:lattice-affine-box-refusal`). Direct affine-expression replay costs 219,119 Work for either torus fixture with 3,440 retained bytes, 60,913/59,633 for phase wrap/straddle with 1,560 bytes, and 83,132 for scalar LC with 2,104 bytes (`test:integration:lattice-direct-coordinate-costs`). Literal `N`/`N−1` checks include 37 prior Work (`test:integration:lattice-direct-coordinate-work`). Direct phase replay is cheaper than transporting E, while direct torus replay is more expensive; the producer's route choice preserves the distinct judgments of (`rule:cas:lattice-affine-box`), (`rule:cas:lattice-coordinate-box`).

**Theorem (Certified lattice tuple selection)** · `thm:cas:decision-lattice-floor-selection`

Formal schema `decision.lattice-floor-selection`, version 1. The original lattice target belongs to the unique half-open cell indexed by the exact ordered Integer tuple. Required premises: Identical checked affine cover, complete original-target affine enclosure, exact tuple membership in every declared index range and strict whole-box floor cuts, retaining all qualified cover hypotheses. Standing: Lemma; instances are CAS-checked.

**Assumption (Selected lattice tuple)** · `assum:cas:hyp-lattice-selection`

Formal hypothesis `hyp.lattice-selection`, version 1. The original lattice target belongs to the half-open cell indexed by the exact ordered Integer tuple. Required premises: Complete affine cover question, original target and ordered Integer literals; a retained statement alone confers no certified tuple-selection authority. Standing: Hypothesis; every undischarged use remains conditional.

**Rule (Named original-target tuple selection)** · `rule:cas:lattice-tuple-selection`

`Cas::prove_lattice_selection` checks one exact ordered Integer tuple against the identical retained affine cover of (`rule:cas:lattice-cover`), with every coordinate in its declared range. It replays either original-target enclosure form of (`rule:cas:lattice-affine-box`), (`rule:cas:lattice-coordinate-box`), then checks each included lower face and strictly excluded upper face before publishing the finite theorem of (`thm:cas:decision-lattice-floor-selection`). Fresh inspection retains the complete cover premise, qualified parameter assumptions and actual Machin dependency (`test:integration:lattice-tuple-selection`). No cell enumeration or executable Integer conversion participates in selection.

**Rule (Original identity and atomic tuple refusals)** · `rule:cas:lattice-tuple-identity`

Expected-claim replay matches the entire original question and target, even when the candidate tuple is unchanged (`test:integration:lattice-tuple-expected-target`). Missing coordinates, Real literals offered as Integers, a changed original target and excluded parameter-range values refuse at their actual typed-role or range checker (`test:integration:lattice-tuple-refusals`). Whole-box torus and phase straddles preserve graph counts and canonical snapshots without publishing a tuple prefix (`test:integration:lattice-tuple-straddles`). These checks constrain the selected proposition of (`rule:cas:lattice-tuple-selection`) independently of producer choice.

**Example (Torus, wrap, scalar and grid tuple fixtures)** · `ex:cas:lattice-tuple-fixtures`

For `B = 2π·I_2` and `q = (20,10)`, the narrow original box `[19,20] × [9,10]` selects `(3,1)`, whereas `[18,21] × [9,10]` refuses the whole tuple. The phase target `5π/2` in `[15/2,8]` selects 1, the original `10/Pi` box selects 3, and the rational quantization grid selects `(2,3)` (`test:integration:lattice-tuple-selection`), (`test:integration:lattice-tuple-straddles`). For `q = 2π`, transporting `[6,13/2]` cannot select; independently replaying the exact original affine expression as singleton `{1}` selects its owned face (`test:integration:lattice-tuple-direct-selection`). A literal surrogate cannot replace that expression (`test:integration:lattice-tuple-direct-refusal`), preserving the distinct evidence contracts of (`rule:cas:lattice-affine-box`), (`rule:cas:lattice-coordinate-box`).

**Rule (Portable cover identity sharing)** · `rule:cas:lattice-tuple-wire-sharing`

The new portable tuple proposition references the complete cover proposition already retained by its checked premise, rather than encoding that question twice. Local structural identity and caller expected claims still include every original cover field, and fresh replay still requires the independently checked matching cover application (`rule:cas:lattice-tuple-selection`), (`test:integration:lattice-tuple-expected-target`). The original coordinate and coefficient certificates remain explicit replay evidence; statement sharing supplies no authority by itself (`assum:cas:hyp-lattice-selection`). Existing statement encodings and unrelated cost paths are unchanged.

**Example (Tuple proof and fresh inspection costs)** · `ex:cas:lattice-tuple-costs`

After prerequisite cover admission, original-box tuple proof/fresh inspection cost 404,211/1,071,050 Work for the narrow torus, 398,592/978,609 for phase wrap, 313,199/758,686 for scalar LC and 335,639/876,456 for the quantization grid; their witnesses contain 4,004, 4,120, 3,575 and 3,745 bytes respectively (`test:integration:lattice-tuple-work`). Direct-H proof/fresh inspection costs are 473,783/1,173,089 for torus, 303,727/884,277 for phase wrap, 295,200/857,936 for the owned wrap face, 305,136/759,867 for scalar LC and 334,530/907,345 for grid, with 4,113, 4,042, 3,872, 3,619 and 3,862 witness bytes (`test:integration:lattice-tuple-direct-work`). Each owner has literal `N`/`N−1` boundaries including 37 prior Work, unchanged caller limits and atomic graph refusal, enforcing (`rule:cas:lattice-tuple-identity`); existing scalar pins remain unchanged (`ex:cas:lattice-selection-scalar-baseline`).

**Example (Measured sealing split and remaining wire cost)** · `ex:cas:lattice-tuple-sealing`

Temporary exclusive probes for the cited torus witness measured 13,978 Work in two application copies, 1,456 in DAG lookup keys, 18,836 in record serialization, 120,192 in the retained-record footprint charge, 54,829 in canonical ordering and 4,004 in the envelope: 213,295 total across 74 records. The scalar nonnegative `10/Pi` comparison measured 3,736, 423, 4,864, 42,432, 8,608 and 1,562 respectively: 61,625 Work across 36 records (`ex:cas:lattice-selection-scalar-baseline`). Cover citation reduces torus proof Work from 406,440 to 404,211 and bytes from 4,080 to 4,004 (`rule:cas:lattice-tuple-wire-sharing`), (`ex:cas:lattice-tuple-costs`). Across these fixtures, record count grows about 2.1 times, footprint about 2.8 times and ordering about 6.4 times; this measured superlinearity belongs to the shared wire builder and remains a separate optimization obligation. It is not a general asymptotic bound.

**Requirement (Remaining tuple result owners)** · `req:cas:lattice-tuple-results`

The implemented theorem of (`rule:cas:lattice-tuple-selection`) does not yet supply typed undecided handoffs, an immutable paired representative descriptor with its checked reconstruction identity, or coordinate capacity consumers. Those remain the root design's undecided-handoff and exact-selected-outputs obligations. Nearest-point admission, scalar specialization, physical bindings and live publication retain their separate owners; the fixture claims of (`ex:cas:lattice-tuple-fixtures`) concern mathematical floor selection only.
